---
import EnglishHeader from '../../../components/EnglishHeader.astro'; import EnglishFooter from '../../../components/EnglishFooter.astro'; const base=import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/,''); const images=`${base}/images/bikes/cycles-france-sport`; const facts=[['Dating','c. 1916–1920'],['Marque','Cycles France-Sport'],['Origin','Toulouse, France'],['Material','Steel'],['Weight','c. 14 kg'],['Gearing','Reversible rear wheel, 2 sprockets'],['Condition','Restored with patina preserved']];
---
<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><meta name="description" content="A Cycles France-Sport racing bicycle from around 1919, found in a barn north of Toulouse."/><title>Cycles France-Sport c. 1919 · The Evolution of Bikes</title><link rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}/styles/global.css`}/></head><body><EnglishHeader current="bikes" danishPath="/cykler/cycles-france-sport/"/><main><article class="bike-page">
<header class="bike-hero"><div class="bike-kicker"><a href={`${base}/en/bikes/`}>← The collection</a><span>France · c. 1919</span></div><h1>Cycles<br/><em>France-Sport</em></h1><p class="bike-lead">The bicycle that waited for almost 100 years at the back of a barn north of Toulouse.</p></header>
<figure class="bike-hero-image"><img src={`${images}/hero.webp`} alt="Cycles France-Sport as found, complete and with clear patina"/><figcaption>Before restoration — dusty, complete and with its history still visible in the metal.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="bike-intro"><p class="section-label">Found</p><div class="bike-prose bike-prose-large"><p>In 2019 it stood at the back of a barn north of Toulouse, beneath thick dust but surprisingly complete and sound.</p><p>It is provisionally dated to about 1916–1920. The France-Sport marque was associated with René Bancal in Toulouse and had been registered by 1909.</p></div></section>
<dl class="bike-facts" aria-label="Bicycle facts">{facts.map(([label,value])=><div><dt>{label}</dt><dd>{value}</dd></div>)}</dl>
<section class="bike-story-split"><div class="bike-prose"><p class="section-label">A name in brass</p><h2>Who was Alfred?</h2><p>A small homemade brass plate was wrapped around the seatpost. Its handwriting includes the name <strong>Alfred</strong> and a reference to Alba near Réalville in Tarn-et-Garonne.</p><p>We do not yet know who Alfred was. The plate nevertheless preserves a human trace: the name of someone who probably rode the bicycle.</p></div><div class="detail-duo"><figure class="detail-figure"><img src={`${images}/owner-plate.webp`} alt="Homemade brass owner plate" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>The handwritten plate remained on the bicycle.</figcaption></figure><figure class="detail-figure detail-inset"><img src={`${images}/owner-plate-detail.webp`} alt="Detail showing the name Alfred" loading="lazy"/></figure></div></section>
<section class="artifact-band"><figure><img src={`${images}/flyer.webp`} alt="Worn French fête flyer found inside the handlebar" loading="lazy"/></figure><div class="bike-prose"><p class="section-label">Hidden in the handlebar</p><h2>A flyer for a spring fête</h2><p>When restoration began in Denmark, a French fête flyer emerged from inside the handlebar.</p><p>It states <em>dimanche 31 mars</em> — Sunday 31 March. The best provisional calendar match is 1957. If correct, the bicycle was already around forty years old when the paper was hidden there.</p><p class="uncertainty">The dating is provisional because the year has not survived on the fragment.</p></div></section>
<section class="bike-story-split reverse"><figure class="detail-figure badge-figure"><img src={`${images}/badge.webp`} alt="Original enamelled France-Sport head badge" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>The original badge in French colours.</figcaption></figure><div class="bike-prose"><p class="section-label">The mechanics</p><h2>Two gears — and a pause by the road</h2><p>The reversible rear wheel has a sprocket on each side: a higher gear for level roads and a lower one for hills.</p><p>Changing gear meant stopping, loosening and reversing the wheel, then fitting it again. Simple and robust, it marked a useful step beyond a single fixed ratio.</p></div></section>
<section class="restoration-note"><p class="section-label">Restoration</p><blockquote>Restored — but not made new.</blockquote><p>The original surfaces, patina and signs of use were retained because they are part of the story.</p></section>
<section class="period-note"><span>The period</span><p>Philippe Thys was among cycling’s leading names when this bicycle was built, winning the Tour in 1913, 1914 and 1920. He did not ride this France-Sport, but his bicycles belonged to the same technical and working world.</p></section>
<nav class="bike-bottom-nav" aria-label="Bicycle navigation"><a href={`${base}/en/bikes/`}>← All bicycles</a><a href={`${base}/en/bikes/wonder-saint-etienne/`}>Wonder Saint-Étienne →</a></nav></article></main><EnglishFooter/></body></html>
